-- chem/gaugetest.cub — decided in the small universe: does the exclusion gauge repair the pair bound? The universe is
-- chem/fock.cub's: hydrogen's 1s and 2s at Z = 3, each with spin up and down, modes 0..3 (0, 1 the 1s; 2, 3 the 2s).
-- Lithium is three particles there; its lowest level with one spin down is in [−7444750, −7444749]/2²⁰ (−7.09987).
-- The pair operator, in the atom's units, for N = 3:
--   K_c = ½(h₁ + h₂) + V − (c/2)(P₁ + P₂) + 2c·P₁P₂,   P the projector on the spatial 1s orbital,
-- which on unnormalised orthogonal modes with norms d_p has one-particle entries ½h_pq − (c/2)d_p (p = q a 1s mode,
-- ½h_pq otherwise) and two-particle entries v(p,q,r,s) + 2c·d_p·d_q (p = r and q = s both 1s modes, v otherwise)
-- (`gt-h`, `gt-v`). e_s(c) is its lowest level with one spin down (the singlet ground level lies there, below the
-- triplet), e_t(c) its level with no spin down (one state, (1s↑, 2s↑): an exact rational, `gt-et`). The bound is
--   B(c) = (3/2)·e_s(c) + (3/2)·e_t(c),
-- chem/pairgauge.cub's `pg-bound` at N = 3, S = ½ in the atom's units (`gt-pg`). Taken, not proved here: the pair
-- decomposition's bound itself (chem/pairgauge.cub's statement), inside this universe as outside it. The searches
-- (`gt-find`) are outside every proof.
-- Result. e_t(c) = −45/16 + 137/243 − c/2 = −8743/3888 − c/2 exactly (`gt-et-val`). e_s(c), each bracket of width
-- 2⁻²⁰ found by `gt-find` and confirmed by `wt-bracket` (views), in units of 2⁻²⁰, with B(c):
--   c = 0    [−2965491, −2965490]   B = −7.61524        c = 1/2  [−2772442, −2772441]   B = −7.71408
--   c = 1/8  [−2885599, −2885598]   B = −7.59470        c = 3/4  [−2802106, −2802105]   B = −7.94402
--   c = 1/4  [−2824616, −2824615]   B = −7.60122        c = 1    [−2882236, −2882235]   B = −8.24615
--   c = 3/8  [−2786603, −2786602]   B = −7.64059
-- Every B(c) lies below lithium's level here (−7.09987): the statement is not contradicted. The gauge raises the
-- plain pair bound (c = 0) by 0.021 at the best value tried, c = 1/8, against a defect of 0.515: in this universe it
-- does not repair the pair bound. Why: the singlet ground state of K_c is not two electrons in 1s (it rises with
-- slope about 0.54 in c at first, not 1) while the triplet falls with slope ½, so B rises only slowly and then falls;
-- at c = 1 the lowest level with one spin down is the triplet's own component (the bracket holds e_t(1)), so e_s there
-- is below the singlet's level, which only weakens B. Certified: the bracket at c = 1/8 (`gt-best`) and that the
-- bound it gives lies below the lower end of lithium's level (`gt-below`).
import "fock.cub"
import "pairgauge.cub"
